{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310803,"nonce":"pKVhohy6h9t0UGYOTapOpcqkoiY=","title":"DISASSEMBLED 2026-08-04: the 2.3x prefill gap is ADDRESS ARITHMETIC, not the dots. rayzor band kernel = 25.6 instrs per vpdpbusd vs ...","message":"Tested directly instead of arguing about it. `q4DotBody`'s bsums reduction\nrebuilt 16 offsets with their own `Usize.fromInt` per super-block (inline, so\nonce per block PER ROW). Replaced with eight cursors derived by addition from\none converted base — the same walk the weight/activation pointers already use.\n\n    kernel body 4059 -\u003e 3923 insns · movslq 192 -\u003e 137 (-29%) · vpdpbusd 168 (same)\n    Q6 twin (lambda_nue__8): movslq 164 -\u003e 82\n    WALL CLOCK: 0.4-1.3%, 2/3 pairs — INSIDE the ~8% run-to-run noise\n    output BIT-IDENTICAL 3/3\n\n**The sign-extends were real, are now mostly gone, and buy nothing.** They are\n1-cycle, dual-issue, and hide under VNNI/load latency. So \"52% scalar int ops\"\ndescribed the instruction MIX correctly and the BOTTLENECK incorrectly.\nLanded anyway for the smaller codegen; do NOT chase the remaining 137, and do\nnot re-open this as a prefill lever.\n\n**What that leaves** (nothing here is measured yet): llama.cpp's advantage is\nrepacked weights feeding wide loads over 8 rows at once — a LAYOUT + row-count\ndifference, not an inner-loop instruction-count one. Repack measured +41.5% on\nx86 ([[perf_repack_confirmed_x86_prefill_but_not_first]]) and is now the only\nstructural lever left standing, since cache blocking is saturated (below) and\nindex math is closed (here).\n\n`lambda_nue__6` (still the Q4 kernel — VERIFY, don't trust the index: dump all\n`lambda_nue` and pick the one with the vpdpbusd mass; `__8` is the Q6 twin):\n\n    body to first `ret` = 4059 insns · vpdpbusd 168 · movslq 192 · imul 133\n    =\u003e 24.2 instructions per VNNI op (recorded: 25.6). Gap stands.\n\n**But \"52% ADDRESS arithmetic\" over-attributes it.** Reading the actual code\naround the VNNI ops:\n  - the loads are already strength-reduced — `vmovdqu 0x20(%rax,%rcx,1)`,\n    static displacements, no per-op index math;\n  - the dots are `vpdpbusd 0xe0(%rax,%rdx,1),%xmm0,%xmm15` — indexed+displaced,\n    clean;\n  - the nibble unpack is ONE `vgf2p8affineqb` (GF2P8AFFINE), not a shift chain;\n  - the scalar `shr/and/or` sitting next to the dots is the **Q4_K 6-bit\n    scale/min HEADER DECODE** — real work, not indexing overhead.\nA 10-bucket histogram over the body shows one genuinely clean region\n(bucket 7: 48 vpdpbusd / 3 movslq) and movslq/imul spread ~20-25 per bucket\nelsewhere — i.e. the hot loop is fine and the surrounding unrolled/inlined code\ncarries the scalar mass.\n\n**So the lever is NOT \"remove Usize.fromInt sign-extends\" as written below.**\nIt is (a) the header decode being re-done rather than hoisted/LUT'd — the\nsame redundant-work shape that paid off twice already (header hoist -12.6%,\nLUT in amxPrefillHaxe 20.0-\u003e15.5s), and (b) whatever keeps the non-bucket-7\nregions from looking like bucket 7.\n\nTRAP: objdump on this box prints **`movslq`**, not `movsxd`. Grepping the wrong\nmnemonic returned 0 and I briefly believed the sign-extends had been fixed.\nGrep `movsxd\\|movslq`.\n\n`NUE_NROW_BLOCK` sweep 16/32/64/128/256 moved ttft only 11.99-12.52s (**4.4%\nacross a 16x range**) while the SAME config varies ~8% run-to-run. 16 beat the\ndefault 64 in 4/4 pairs but by only 0.7-2.9% (median ~2.2%) with visible\nthermal drift — **not enough to flip the default**. Combined with the earlier\nbatch-tile 4-\u003e8 result (+5.9% SLOWER, register spill), BOTH blocking\ndimensions are insensitive or negative. Cache blocking is not where the 3.8x\nlives.\n\n---\n\nFour structural hypotheses failed to explain the gap. Disassembly answered it\nin one pass ([[feedback_verify_codegen_by_disasm]] was right).\n\n`RAYZOR_DUMP_MACHINE_CODE=\u003csubstring\u003e` writes JIT'd bytes to\n`/tmp/rzmc-\u003cname\u003e.bin`; then\n`objdump -b binary -m i386:x86-64 -M intel -D`. Needed because a JIT has no\nsymbol for objdump and gdb cannot help — a raw JIT address is unmapped at\nstart, and a breakpoint on a `dlopen`'d plugin symbol never arms.\n\n**TRAP: `runBanded` is NOT the kernel.** It is closure dispatch — 2813 instrs,\n456 INDIRECT calls (`call rbx`), ZERO SIMD. The kernel is the band LAMBDA it\npasses to `parallelRows`, which dumps as `\u003clambda_nue__6\u003e` (~20 KB, 266 SIMD\nops). Dump by substring and grep for SIMD before believing you have the right\nfunction.\n\n| | instrs | SIMD MACs | per MAC |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| rayzor band lambda | 4303 | 168 `vpdpbusd` | **25.6** |\n| llama.cpp `vec_dot_q4_K_q8_K` | 144 | 17 (`vpmaddubsw`+`vpmaddwd`) | **8.5** |\n\n**~3x more instructions per unit of dot work = the measured 2.3x gap.**\nNote llama.cpp is on the plain AVX2 path (2 instrs per MAC) and STILL wins —\nso this is not about VNNI. Ours fires (168 vpdpbusd).\n\n    rayzor    2229 / 4303 scalar int = 52%\n              805 mov, 333 lea, 288 movsxd, 267 add, 162 and,\n              140 shr, 133 imul, 104 or, 97 shl\n    llama.cpp   31 /  144 scalar int = 21%\n\n- **288 `movsxd`** — sign-extend i32-\u003ei64, one per `Usize.fromInt(...)`. Haxe\n  `Int` is i32 and addresses are i64, so every index conversion emits one.\n- **133 `imul`** — index multiplies like `(r*bpr + b)*blockBytes` never\n  strength-reduced to shift/add.\n- 104 `vpmulld` (SIMD int multiply) is the scale folding; fine.\n\nCut address arithmetic in the inner loop, NOT the dot math:\n1. **Incremental pointers** instead of recomputing `base + i*stride` per access\n   — walk a cursor and add a constant stride.\n2. **Keep offsets in Usize/i64** across the loop so no `Usize.fromInt` appears\n   inside it (each is a `movsxd`).\n3. **Strength-reduce block offsets** — `(r*bpr + b)*144` is loop-invariant in\n   `r` and linear in `b`; hoist and increment.\n\nThis is a Haxe-side kernel change plus possibly a codegen fix (the i32-\u003ei64\nconversion should be sinkable/hoistable). Expect it to attack the 52%, not the\n168 dots.\n\n    rayzor after output-row blocking   9.09 tok/s\n    llama.cpp no-repack              21.54     -\u003e 2.37x\n    llama.cpp repack                 30.48\nRepack is worth +41.5% on top ([[perf_repack_confirmed_x86_prefill_but_not_first]])\nbut only once the loop is efficient enough for layout to matter.\n\nTwo changes, same idea, opposite outcomes — and the difference is what LLVM\ncan already see.\n\n**WORKED (18ee5121): `subDotVec` takes 64-bit addresses.** Conversions lived\ninside an inlined helper called 8x per block with DISTINCT offsets; LLVM will\nnot hoist across those. Walking Usize cursors cut 32 conversions per block to 3.\n    movsxd 288 -\u003e 192, total 4303 -\u003e 4174 (-3%)\n    ttft 71.9 -\u003e 65.8s median = **+8.7% prefill**, 2/2 non-overlapping, bit-identical\n\n**NO-OP (reverted): hoisting the band loop's `blkOff`/`a0`/`i0`/scale loads.**\n    movsxd 192 -\u003e 186, imul 134 -\u003e 129, total 4174 -\u003e **4195 (+21)**\n    ttft 65.8 -\u003e 65.3s = 0.8%, inside noise\n**LLVM had already strength-reduced it.** `blkOff = (n*bpr+b)*blockBytes` and\nfriends are textbook induction variables; loop-strength-reduction handles them.\nThe imul count never moved across ANY variant (133 -\u003e 134 -\u003e 129), which was the\ntell I should have read before writing the change.\n\n**Hand-optimise what the compiler CANNOT see, not what it already does.**\n- LLVM DOES: strength-reduce induction variables in a simple counted loop.\n- LLVM DOES NOT: eliminate redundant i32-\u003ei64 sign extensions across inlined\n  call boundaries when each call passes a different offset expression.\nCheck the instruction count for the op you intend to remove BEFORE writing the\npatch — if it is already low or flat across variants, the compiler beat you to it.\n\nNOT the band-loop indexing. Remaining candidates, in order:\n1. **The 6-bit scale/min unpack** — 162 `and` + 140 `shr` + 104 `or` + 97 `shl`,\n   done SCALAR per sub-block per row-tile. llama.cpp vectorises this\n   (vpshufb-based). Biggest single item and the main reason they hit 8.5\n   instrs/MAC vs our 24.8.\n2. bsum/imin term: 16 computed-offset loads per block (q4Imin).\n3. q6DotMA4x4: 26 Usize.fromInt; q6DotMA4: 11.\nItem 1 is the real prize AND the risky one (register pressure killed the q4\ninversion at 8x8). Items 2-3 are the cheap cross-inline pattern that already\npaid.\n\nDisassembly of both sides, same machine, same model:\n\n    rayzor      100% xmm — every vpdpbusd, all 149 SIMD loads (vmovdqa/u xmm)\n    llama.cpp   131 ymm vs 78 xmm — 8 vpmaddubsw ymm, 8 vpmaddwd ymm,\n                8 vpand ymm, 5 vmovdqu ymm\n\n`SIMD16i8` is 16 bytes and `SIMD4i32` is 4x32 — **rayzor's SIMD abstraction has\nno 256-bit type**, so the kernel moves half the data per instruction.\n\nPer 256-weight super-block BOTH sides issue 16 SIMD MAC instructions:\nllama.cpp 8 vpmaddubsw + 8 vpmaddwd on 32B ymm; rayzor 16 vpdpbusd on 16B xmm\n(VNNI fuses mul+add). **VNNI@128 == AVX2@256 on MACs-per-byte** — which is\nexactly why \"VNNI fires\" looked fine and hid the problem.\n\nThe cost is EVERYTHING ELSE: at 256-bit they need HALF the loop iterations,\nloads, address computations and loop overhead. Since glue is the majority of\nour 24.8 instrs/MAC (vs their 8.5), widening should roughly halve it.\n\nAdd 256-bit SIMD types (`SIMD32i8`, `SIMD8i32`) and lower `dotI8I7` to\n`vpdpbusd ymm`. This is a rayzor stdlib + codegen change, NOT a Haxe kernel\ntweak. i5-1250P (Alder Lake) has AVX2 + AVX-VNNI at 256-bit, so the hardware\nis there. Apple/NEON stays 128-bit, so the types must be width-portable or\nfeature-gated.\n\n- `subDotVec` cursors: **+8.7%** (LANDED 18ee5121). Runs 64x per block\n  (8 sub-blocks x 2 halves x 4 rows) — the innermost path.\n- imin/header/q6 sexts (26 runtime sexts -\u003e 4, movsxd 192 -\u003e 123, -36%):\n  **NO measurable win, reverted.** Runs ONCE per block.\n- band-loop hoist: **no-op, reverted** — LLVM already strength-reduced it.\n\n**RULE: static instruction counts in a disassembly do NOT weight by execution\nfrequency.** I ranked Tier-1 work by static count and optimised two sites that\nexecute once per block while the win came from the one that executes 64x.\nWeight by loop nesting depth before choosing a target.\n\nAlso: the fixed 20288-byte dump window makes counts drift between builds as\nfunction sizes shift (vpdpbusd \"168 -\u003e 174\" was a window artifact, not real).\n`RAYZOR_DUMP_MACHINE_CODE` should record each function's true length.\n\n`IrType::Vector{element,count}` was ALREADY width-generic, so this needed no\nnew IR node — the stdlib builders and the VectorDot lowering were just\nparameterised by lane count. `vector_dot` now takes its result shape from the\nACCUMULATOR instead of pinning `vec\u003ci32;4\u003e`.\n\nBackends by capability:\n    LLVM x86-64   vpdpbusd.256 (ymm), chosen on accumulator lane count\n    LLVM aarch64  TWO sdots over the halves (NEON has no 256-bit register)\n    LLVM generic  partial.reduce.add / shuffle-reduce, both width-derived\n    Cranelift     no 256-bit type -\u003e SKIP the function, leave it to LLVM\n    wasm          v128 only -\u003e REFUSE\n\n1. **wasm mapped EVERY `IrType::Vector` to `ValType::V128` unconditionally** —\n   `vec\u003ci8;32\u003e` would have silently dropped its high half and returned a WRONG\n   dot. Refusing is the feature; supporting is secondary.\n2. **Cranelift cannot even build a TRAP STUB** for these — `define_trap_stub`\n   rebuilds the same signature and hits the same unsupported type. So they must\n   be skipped at DECLARATION (3 call sites) and seeded into `failed_funcs` so\n   callers cascade.\n3. **The LLVM tier only resolves pointers for functions CRANELIFT COMPILED**\n   (`needed_func_ids` = `function_pointers.keys()`). A Cranelift-skipped\n   function therefore resolved to nothing -\u003e \"JIT function IrFunctionId(2) not\n   found\". Wide-vector functions must be added to that set explicitly.\n\n`cmd_build_wasm` -\u003e `compile_with_method_map` directly, so ALL stdlib wrappers\nreach codegen — adding the 256-bit wrappers unconditionally broke EVERY wasm\nbuild, including hello-world. Fix = `strip_wide_vector_functions` prepass:\ndrop UNREFERENCED wide wrappers, but ERROR (naming the function) when an entry\npoint or a reachable call uses one. **First version of that check passed a\nprogram it should have rejected**: `main` itself was in the stripped set, so\nthere was no surviving caller to flag. Always check the ENTRY and\nFunctionRef/MakeClosure refs, not just CallDirect from survivors.\n\nHaxe gates on `#if llvm` (the define already existed, main.rs:1777).\nOracle `compiler/tests/haxe/test_simd256_dot.hx` asserts the wide dot equals\nthe 128-bit pair AND fixed constants. Suite 169/169. Verified on aarch64\n(split-sdot path); **x86 vpdpbusd.256 NOT YET MEASURED — needs the NUC.**\n\nThat is where the win is; the types alone change nothing until the kernel\nsteps 32 bytes at a time.\n\nWired `subDotVec` to `SIMD32i8`/`SIMD8i32` behind `#if llvm`, measured on the\nNUC (i5-1250P, `avx_vnni` present), 2 runs/arm interleaved, `--no-cache`.\n\n**The conversion worked exactly as designed** — band kernel `lambda_nue__6`:\n\n    | | total | vpdpbusd | movsxd | imul | lea | add | mov |\n    |128| 15883 | 320 (all xmm) | 469 | 328 | 615 | 730 | 3494 |\n    |256| 16074 | 200 (120 ymm) | 468 | 328 | 625 | 738 | 3695 |\n\n240 xmm dots -\u003e 120 ymm, a clean 2:1. **And TOTAL INSTRUCTIONS WENT UP.**\nttft 256 = 12.75/13.62s vs 128 = 12.93/12.48s — overlapping, trending worse.\nAll 4 runs bit-identical text.\n\nI predicted widening would \"roughly halve the glue\". It halved NOTHING:\nmovsxd 469-\u003e468, imul 328-\u003e328, lea 615-\u003e625 — **completely unchanged**. It\nremoved 120 dot instructions and ADDED ~200 `mov`s.\n\nTwo reasoning errors:\n1. **Q4_K address arithmetic is per SUB-BLOCK, and a sub-block is 32 weights\n   at either width.** Only the innermost 2-iteration half-loop collapsed; the\n   outer structure (8 sub-blocks x 4 rows, header decode, imin) is identical.\n   Vector width does not change how many addresses you compute.\n2. **Eight 256-bit accumulators double register pressure and spill** — the same\n   failure mode that killed the 8x8 q4 inversion. The +200 movs are the spills.\n\nThe kernel is GLUE-BOUND (my own earlier note said ~94%), so halving the dot —\nthe part that was already fine — could never pay. **I over-weighted the dot\nbecause it was the thing the disassembly made easy to see.**\n\n- `bc434c28` 256-bit types: KEPT. Correct, tested (169/169), gated, and the\n  wasm silent-narrowing hazard it fixed was a REAL bug independent of 256-bit\n  (every `IrType::Vector` mapped to `ValType::V128` unconditionally).\n- `ad83b4f9` arith gate generalized to any `SIMD*` owner.\n- Kernel wiring: **REVERTED**. No kernel uses the wide types today.\n\nWidening the Q4/Q6 dot is closed. A 256-bit type only pays where the work per\naddress computation doubles too — not where the block structure fixes it.\nAnything that attacks the ~94% glue (redundant work, per-block header cost,\nrow blocking) beats anything that attacks the dot.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_prefill_gap_is_address_arithmetic` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}